WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.900
Spider-Man is headed back to Sony, and the
guys who helped bring him into the Marvel

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:08.460
Cinematic Universe are disappointed, but not
surprised.

00:00:08.769 --> 00:00:13.060
During a recent conversation with The Daily
Beast, Avengers: Endgame directors Joe and

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:17.460
Anthony Russo shared their thoughts on the
web-slinger's unceremonious exit from the

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.440
biggest film franchise in history.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:24.849
The pair related that while they and Marvel
Studios head honcho Kevin Feige had been absolutely

00:00:24.849 --> 00:00:30.340
driven to bring Spidey into their universe,
the shared custody deal with Sony was an extremely

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:35.989
difficult one to get done, so difficult that
negotiations to extend it falling apart didn't

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:38.660
register to them as being terribly surprising.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:44.020
Peter Parker appeared in the MCU for the first
time in the Russo Brothers' second Marvel

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.120
flick: Captain America: Civil War.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:50.100
From the beginning of the project's development,
the directors were adamant that the story

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:55.200
should include Spider-Man, despite the obvious
stumbling block of Sony holding the film rights.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:56.660
Anthony Russo said,

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.360
"We were extremely passionate about it.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:04.100
This is something we really wanted to happen,
and fought a long time internally at Marvel

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.140
to make it happen."

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.420
There were, of course, contingencies in the
very likely event that a deal wouldn't be

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:10.920
struck.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:15.560
Black Panther, who was also introduced in
Civil War, would have had his role greatly

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:20.250
expanded in the event that Spidey couldn't
come out to play, and several other narrative

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.200
elements would have been different as well.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.770
The movie's script went through several iterations
as all of the involved parties were attempting

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:30.670
to reach an agreement, and poor Feige was
right in the middle of it all, attempting

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:35.020
to juggle the creative and business sides
of his job in a way that he hadn't been asked

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:35.780
to do before.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:37.400
Joe Russo said,

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:38.900
"Kevin went through a lot.

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:43.240
There were a lot of ups and downs, and he
kept walking into our office and we'd go,

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:48.370
'Look, we've got to do it with [Sony],' and
he'd go, 'OK, I'll figure it out,' and walk

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:49.140
back into his.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.100
He was looking for the way out.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.000
He wanted to open that door and have us go,
'We figured it out!

00:01:55.009 --> 00:01:59.530
We don't need Spider-Man!' because it's a
lot of work to get two major corporations

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:03.680
to play nice with each other, and the fact
that it happened at all, we should all be

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.540
dancing and celebrating that we got that little
bit of time."

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:08.640
Anthony Russo agreed, saying,

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:14.130
"I think that's why Joe and I are not so devastated
or surprised that there's been a falling out,

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:16.760
because it was so hard to make it happen in
the first place."

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:22.300
The Russos make an excellent point; the fact
that we got two solo and three team-up adventures

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:27.160
featuring Spider-Man in the Marvel Cinematic
Universe is in itself a minor miracle.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.940
But this doesn't do much to ease the broken
hearts of the franchise's fans, who had wanted

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:36.160
Tom Holland's Peter Parker to stick around
in the MCU for just a little longer.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.900
"I don't want to go, I don't want to go, sir,
please!

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:40.740
Please, I don't want to go.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:41.900
I don't want to go."

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.480
The matter, though, appears to have been settled.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:51.020
Sony Pictures chairman and CEO Tony Vinciquerra
threw cold water on any remaining dreams fans

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:55.640
might have with remarks he recently made at
Variety's Entertainment and Technology Summit,

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.580
saying, quote, "For the moment the door is
closed."

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:04.400
Vinciquerra also expressed the extremely optimistic
view that Spider-Man will do "just fine" in

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:08.730
the Sony Universe of Marvel Characters, without
help from Marvel Studios.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.120
Because, sure!

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.560
Who needs the Avengers when you have Morbius
and Silver Sable?

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:16.660
"What is this place?!"

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:22.360
To be fair, Venom did do pretty darn well
at the box office, although it got nowhere

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:26.040
near the critical love that even the worst-received
MCU films have.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:31.650
We suppose it'll be cool to see Tom Hardy's
Eddie Brock and Holland's Peter Parker squaring

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:37.480
off, but let's face facts: after the debacle
that was the rebooted Amazing Spider-Man series,

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.940
it was obvious that Sony had completely lost
its way with Spidey.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:46.450
The MCU resurrected his box office fortunes,
giving Sony Pictures, which reaped all the

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:52.510
profits from web-head's MCU-set solo adventures,
not only its top-performing Spider-Man film,

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:57.920
but the highest-grossing flick in its history
with the wall-crawler's MCU swan song, Spider-Man:

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:58.860
Far From Home.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:03.960
Fans may love Holland, but it's hard to imagine
his next appearance in the Sony Universe of

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.880
Marvel Characters will resonate the same way
his appearances in the MCU have.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:12.520
And who knows how Marvel will have to change
things up now that they don't have Spider-man

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:13.240
around?

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:17.380
"I don't think Tony would have done what he
did if he didn't know that you were going

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:18.700
to be here after he was gone."

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:23.220
Of course, there's still time for Sony to
come to its senses and invite Mighty Marvel

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:28.250
back to the bargaining table, but at the moment,
it appears that the studio is intent on learning

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:30.500
what fans don't want, the hard way.

